# Logitech PRO X 60 LIGHTSPEED (046d:c351) on its cable, vendor HID++
# interface -> uaccess for the seat user; and its node of its own behind its
# LIGHTSPEED receiver (40AF, below), which carries what is typed on it too.
# Both ids were read off one on 2026-11-14 with gearbench-ctl report, which
# reads and writes nothing, and Gearbench drove that one on both routes the
# same day.
#
# Interface 02: it holds the vendor collection (usage page 0xFF00) alone,
# HID++'s short and long reports, as that report read from sysfs. Its
# interface 00 is a boot keyboard and its interface 01 carries its pointer,
# consumer and system control reports: read access to either would be a
# keylogger surface, and neither is granted.
#
# Interface 02 carries FN held and let go once a host writes 0x8051 fn 3
# `03 ff`, as Gearbench does at each setup to light the FN keys, while the
# keyboard's custom assignments are on: read on one on 2026-11-14, where
# with them off no report of FN came. It is taken to carry Ctrl, Shift, Alt
# and Super the same way, each press and release, as on the wired G515 TKL
# (70-g515.rules) and the G915 X LIGHTSPEED (70-g915-x.rules), and to keep
# sending them, on its battery, until it is switched off; neither was
# checked on this keyboard. Gearbench reads FN alone. Any
# program of the seat's user can write that and read them, and one that
# holds the node open reads another account's after a user switch; "The
# grant crosses accounts", below, says how to limit the node to a group's
# accounts.
#
# Through the same node a program can also write the keyboard's custom
# assignments (0x8101) so that keys report to the host (0x1B05 event 0) and
# type nothing themselves, read them here and type them back through
# /dev/uinput. A file that set F1 to F4 so made them come on interface 02 of
# the wired G515 TKL on 2026-10-22, and one that set G1 to G9 so made them
# come on the G915 X LIGHTSPEED's on 2026-10-23; this keyboard lists the
# same features and is taken to work the same way, not checked on the
# hardware. So the node can carry what is typed, and a program that holds it
# open can read another account's after a user switch. Gearbench writes this
# keyboard no such file: none of its keys binds.
#
# Matched on properties, as 70-g915.rules is, for the reason it gives. The
# keyboard over Bluetooth has no line here and is not driven.
KERNEL=="hidraw*", SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", ENV{ID_VENDOR_ID}=="046d", ENV{ID_MODEL_ID}=="c351", ENV{ID_USB_INTERFACE_NUM}=="02", TAG+="uaccess"

# The PRO X 60 LIGHTSPEED through its LIGHTSPEED receiver (046d:c547): the
# hidraw node of its own, 0003:046D:40AF.<n>, that the kernel's
# hid-logitech-dj gives a device paired to a c547, as 70-g515.rules says of
# the G515 LIGHTSPEED TKL's 40B4 node. Read on 2026-11-14: the node was bound
# to logitech-hidpp-device and the keyboard answered on it at device index
# 0x01.
#
# This node carries what is typed on the keyboard as well as its HID++
# traffic: its report descriptor, read that day, declares key reports beside
# HID++'s. So with this line installed, any program the user of the active
# seat session runs can read what is typed on the PRO X 60, not only
# Gearbench, which drops those reports unread. The owner granted it on
# 2026-11-14 ("Grant, with a warning"), as the G515 LIGHTSPEED TKL's and the
# G915 X LIGHTSPEED's nodes were granted. It carries no other device's
# reports while nothing else is paired to the receiver (70-g502x.rules says
# why).
#
# The grant crosses accounts, as 70-gearbench-uinput.rules says of
# /dev/uinput's: a program that opened the node keeps reading it after another
# account gets the seat, and so reads what that account types on the PRO X
# 60, a password at the login screen included. On a machine several people
# use, that file's group grant, in a copy of this file in /etc/udev/rules.d/,
# limits the node to the group's accounts. A member then reads it from any
# session, whoever has the seat.
#
# Matched by the HID device's name, as 70-g502x.rules matches the mouse: udev
# gives this node the receiver's properties, so a property match would grant
# the receiver's own interfaces too. KERNELS matches a parent's kernel name,
# which for the paired device is bus:vendor:product.instance.
KERNEL=="hidraw*", SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", KERNELS=="0003:046D:40AF.*", TAG+="uaccess"
